        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "0745cdf0-6be4-5893-9057-634554ff55db",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Blood and soil, Lebensraum\n- Organizations: Nazi Party (NSDAP), Nazi Germany\n- People: Friedrich Ratzel, László Deák\n- Places: Eastern Europe\n- Topics: German nationalism, National Socialism, World War I, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- Blood and soil is a German slogan meaning “Blut und Boden” that expresses a nineteenth-century ideal of a racially defined German national body united with a settlement area.\n- Friedrich Ratzel was a German geographer and ethnographer who lived from August 30, 1844 to August 9, 1904 and was notable for first using the term “Lebensraum” in a sense later adopted by National Socialists.\n- Lebensraum is a modern German concept tied to blood and soil that held that the German people needed to reclaim historically German areas of Eastern Europe for expansion.\n- The Nazi Party (NSDAP) and Nazi Germany supported the most extreme form of the Lebensraum ideology until the end of World War II.\n- László Deák was a Hungarian army officer who lived from July 1, 1891 to November 5, 1946 and served in World War I and World War II.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8cb86f78-1952-5939-a4bd-2fc7caf86d07",
        "text": "Page: Blood and Soil. Sentence: Blood and soil (German: \"Blut und Boden\" ) is a slogan expressing the nineteenth-century German idealization of a racially defined national body (\"blood\") united with a settlement area (\"soil\").\nPage: Blood and Soil. Sentence: The contemporary German concept Lebensraum, the belief that the German people needed to reclaim historically German areas of Eastern Europe into which they could expand, is tied to it.\nPage: Lebensraum. Sentence: The most extreme form of this ideology was supported by the Nazi Party (NSDAP) and Nazi Germany until the end of World War II.\nPage: Friedrich Ratzel. Sentence: Friedrich Ratzel (August 30, 1844 – August 9, 1904) was a German geographer and ethnographer, notable for first using the term \"Lebensraum\" (\"living space\") in the sense that the National Socialists later would.\nPage: László Deák. Sentence: László Deák (1 July 1891 – 5 November 1946) was a Hungarian army officer who served in World War I and World War II.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
